INT. HOSPICE GARDEN ROOM - AFTERNOON

Sun through gauze curtains. A wheelchair by the window. A tray of untouched tea.

LORRAINE PEARCE (70s, shawl, thin) watches birds on a feeder. Her son, GREGORY PEARCE (40s, suit jacket over his arm) sits across from her, shifting.

GREGORY
Mom. Do you want the blanket?

LORRAINE
No.

GREGORY
The window open?

LORRAINE
No.

Silence. The birds scatter and return.

GREGORY
I called Aunt Ruth.

LORRAINE
You shouldn't have.

GREGORY
She wanted to know.

LORRAINE
She always wants to know.

GREGORY
Mom, I --

LORRAINE ^
Gregory, I --

They stop. He gestures: you first. She shakes her head.

GREGORY
Say it.

LORRAINE
I wasn't a good mother.

GREGORY
Don't.

LORRAINE
I'm tired of pretending.

He puts the jacket down. Takes her hand.

GREGORY
You were fine, Mom.

LORRAINE
Fine.

GREGORY
Fine is enough.
